  "summary": "Four channels and forever: music written as data, played by chips.",
  "description": "Tracker music culture grew from ProTracker on the Amiga (1987): musicians composing as pattern data — 4 channels of samples, hex commands, no overdubs — traded freely as MOD/S3M/XM files through scene channels,FTP archives and demoparty competitions. The sound (chunky samples, arpeggiated leads, chip-fried drums) defined 8/16-bit game audio, demoscene soundtracks and the netmusic underground (ModArchive, Mik seri). The 2000s revival turned chiptune into performance culture (Chipzel, Sabrepulse, 8bitpeoples label, Blip Festival) with real Game Boys (LSDJ software) on stage; tracker grammar quietly persists inside DAWs and the demoscene's 64k soundtracks.",
  "culturalContext": "Tracker music culture grew from Amiga ProTracker (1987) into the demoscene's audio backbone and the chiptune revival (Blip Festival, LSDJ Game Boys on stage): music composed as hex pattern data, traded freely as MOD files — the file-trading ethos that prefigured netlabel and remix culture.",
